Self-signed, test-only certificates and keys, made with the openssl CLI. They are never trusted anywhere and protect nothing; tests only read them to check PEM and PKCS#12 parsing.

The PKCS#12 bundles hold ec.pem and ec.sec1.key, with the passphrase `fixture`, made with OpenSSL 3.5:

    openssl pkcs12 -export -in ec.pem -inkey ec.sec1.key -passout pass:fixture -out ec.p12
    openssl pkcs12 -export -legacy -in ec.pem -inkey ec.sec1.key -passout pass:fixture -out ec.legacy.p12
    openssl pkcs12 -export -legacy -in ec.pem -inkey ec.sec1.key -passout pass:fixture \
        -keypbe PBE-SHA1-RC4-128 -certpbe PBE-SHA1-RC4-128 -out ec.rc4.p12
    openssl pkcs12 -export -in ec.pem -inkey ec.sec1.key -passout pass:fixture -nomac -out ec.nomac.p12
    openssl pkcs12 -export -in ec.pem -inkey ec.sec1.key -passout pass:fixture -iter 2000000 -out ec.slow.p12

ec.p12 is PBES2 with AES-256-CBC and an HMAC-SHA256 MAC; ec.legacy.p12 is 3DES for the key, RC2-40 for the certificate and an HMAC-SHA1 MAC; ec.rc4.p12 uses RC4, which is not supported. ec.nomac.p12 has no MAC; ec.slow.p12 uses 2,000,000 iterations, over p12-keystore's limit of 1,000,000.

rsa.alt.pem is a second self-signed certificate for rsa.pkcs8.key, so two certificates share one public key:

    openssl req -x509 -new -key rsa.pkcs8.key -subj /CN=rsa-alt -days 36500 -out rsa.alt.pem

cycle.a.pem (key cycle.key) and cycle.b.pem name each other as issuer, so a chain walk by issuer name must stop when it comes back round. cycle.a.pem was signed by cycle.b.pem, which was signed by a throwaway self-signed cycle-a certificate:

    openssl genpkey -algorithm EC -pkeyopt ec_paramgen_curve:P-256 -out cycle.key
    openssl genpkey -algorithm EC -pkeyopt ec_paramgen_curve:P-256 -out b.key
    openssl req -x509 -new -key cycle.key -subj /CN=cycle-a -days 36500 -out tmp-a.pem
    openssl req -new -key b.key -subj /CN=cycle-b -out b.csr
    openssl x509 -req -in b.csr -CA tmp-a.pem -CAkey cycle.key -days 36500 -out cycle.b.pem
    openssl req -new -key cycle.key -subj /CN=cycle-a -out a.csr
    openssl x509 -req -in a.csr -CA cycle.b.pem -CAkey b.key -days 36500 -out cycle.a.pem
